# nestrs — the settings every profile of the demo reads, production included.
#
# The demo holds no real secret: its keypair is the repo's dev keypair, which
# `auth` signs with in development and in its own suite, and its OAuth
# credentials are fixtures with no provider behind them. All of it is committed,
# so `git clone` then `nestrs run` works with no copy step and no file to prime,
# and none of it sits here: a process declaring no environment runs as production
# and reads this file, and must not trust what anyone with the repo can forge.
# A real app keeps the secret-shaped half in a git-ignored `.env.local`; that is
# what `nestrs new` scaffolds. See https://nestrs.dev/configuration/env-cascade/.
#
# Only OVERRIDES live here, not every setting: anything omitted falls back to the
# in-code default of the owning `#[config]` struct. Real environment variables
# always win over this file.
#
# Every app reads this file. What identifies one app — its port, the audience it
# requires — lives in that app's own `apps/<app>/.env`, read over these under a
# declared development or test profile only; what `auth` signs and registers, in
# its `apps/auth/.env.development` and `.env.test`.
#
# Precedence (highest first):
#   real env  >  apps/<app>/.env.<NESTRS_ENV>.local  >  apps/<app>/.env.local
#   >  apps/<app>/.env.<NESTRS_ENV>  >  apps/<app>/.env
#   >  .env.<NESTRS_ENV>.local  >  .env.local  >  .env.<NESTRS_ENV>  >  .env

# The dev container's services, the credentials its RustFS accepts and the public
# half of the repo's keypair live in `.env.development` and `.env.test`, read only
# under those profiles; a deployment names its own.

# OpenAPI document identity for the `api` app (title, version and description
# are its package's, `apps/api/Cargo.toml`); the license is the demo's own, its
# SPDX identifier.
NESTRS_OPENAPI__LICENSE_NAME=MIT
NESTRS_OPENAPI__LICENSE_IDENTIFIER=MIT
